Z||000006||theogre||04-03-99||11:03 PM||||I tend to agree that adding a header would do more than bigger tubing. I've been looking at this quite a bit lately...... All stock fiero exhausts use fairly poorly constructed weldup manifolds.<p>At the very least, I'd clean up the inside of the manifolds to eliminate the majority of excess pipe on the runners. a dificult task at best on the v6.<p>The 4 Cyl manifold is the worst peice of crap I've seen in over 20 years of dealing with cars. The shared center section is pathetic, and the amount of extra material invading it on the ends of the side runners is unreal.<p>The rest of the 4 cyl exhaust is pretty easy breathing. You aren't going to make any signigant change that will improve it, and keep it 50 state leagal.<p>The rest of the V6 exhaust isn't to awfull eather. Keep in mind..... A Fieros have among the shortest exhaust of any US production car ever made. The Diameter of it's tubes for the distance it travels is pretty big. (Consider that the same tubing diameters are used on the FWD cars with the same motors. and they run the lenght of the car.)<br>For the 4 Cyl it would even be entirely practical to make a header, and use the outlet flange from the stock header as the outlet for the collector. This would alow retention of a stock cat pipe, gasket, and hardware. Making a 4 cyl header using a standard header flange would likely result in allot of leaking, and maybe breakage. The 4 cyl is connected the way it is specifically to allow for engine movement which is considerable even with urathane mounts. A good little header, and a port/polish job, would do more for the 4 cyl than about anything else you can do for the same money.<p>V6 exhaust would flow far better with just a better manifold. Has anyone looked at a cavalier of some other GM car to see if any of those manifolds could be an improvment, and posibly retro fitted to Fiero? Getting a true header on these motors can be done, but it's really tight. (I have looked at 4 cyl's in other applications..... they all use the same lamer manifold.)<p>
Z||000007||PaUL||04-08-99||02:15 PM||||Ive been dreaming about new headers/manifolds for my fiero for years now. All the ones Ive seen cost over $400, too expensive. Someone should come out with cheap headers made out of mild steel costing less than $200, Ive seen them for other cars made by companies like Hooker and Headman, why cant they make them for the fiero?
Z||000008||theogre||04-08-99||02:32 PM||||yes they could make them, but they won't. Not enough volume to justify the tooling. Most of the stuff you do see like that for fiero is being custom built, or produced in small volume. either way they are going to be pricy.
Z||000009||Motorhead||04-08-99||06:10 PM||||I just spent the past couple of days examining the 2.5 exhaust manifold. Since the engine is limited in it's performace by it's weak internal components, I figured if I could come up with a way to help it breath easier at it's lower RPM limitations, I might be able to get a little performance gain without trashing the internals. <p>Intake:<p>Cold/Ram air system with minimal filter restriction.<br>Port and polished intake manifold. With few alterations, the stock intake manifold looks to be pretty free-flowing.<p>Holley big bore TBI, a gi'me!<p>Engine compartment cooling, via induction from the right side of the car. Since the left side is dedicated to engine induction. "Heat soak" right Ogre!<p>Exhaust:<p>Trash the CAT! [No Emissions Inspection where I live]! <p>4-banger header! I know, doesn't exist, but I'm working on that!<p>No CAT means shorter route from head to atmosphere! The only reason the exhaust hangs a right out of the exhaust manifold is to accomadate the catalytic converter.<p>Lastly, the muffler.  GONE! Tip resonators should quiet the ride! Relatively speaking. <p>By the way, I've passed smog inspections with a "gutted catalytic converter! Looks orignal, but actually yielded better emissions numbers than stock!<p>Just my 10 cents!<p><p><br> <p><p>
Z||000010||theogre||04-08-99||10:31 PM||||the drivers side has plenty of room to mount a fan under the grate. if that what you mean by induction. The removal of the "screen" from the grates seems popular with some people. That alone would vent alot of hot air. (I'm gonna try this, but I'm gonna find some extra grates first. That way I still have sock set if I want them.)<p>On pre87's the improved holley TBI gives good results according to what I'm told. I can't use one.... mine's an 87 with it's 700 series TBI. It performs reasonably well.<p>I think a 4cyl header is entirely practical to produce. You need to find a bender, but I don't think the actual bending should be hard. The hard part is making all the runs the same length. I really think this could be done, and retain a stock exhaust. none of the bends are that sharp in the stock system that they are a real problem. You could use all stock pipes, and simply use a test tube instead of the cat. That saves allot of custom work that in the end probly won't do anything. It also gives you a reliable way to see what just adding the header does for the performance. you can test with and w/o the cat that way. you can always play with the rest after that.<p>When ever your trying to develope performance enhancements, it's important to work a little at a time. then you know what make the biggest change. and if the change isn't a good one, what to get rid of.
Z||000011||Patrick||04-09-99||03:05 AM||||Motorhead, if I remember correctly, Hooker does (or did) make headers for the Fiero 4 banger. But they are (or were) <b>very expensive</b>.<p>If you find a practical way to get a few more ponies out of the 2.5, please pass on the tips. I'm stuck with having to leave the "cat" on where I live, but improving the rest of the breathing might be worthwhile trying. I've got to admit though that I'm pretty leary of trying to loosen the bolts that hold the exhaust manifold to the head. If they were to snap off...I don't want to think about it!<p>-Patrick 
